# mac/segments.map — one CODE resource per format family.
#
# NOT used by the default build. It is committed so the measurement below can
# be re-taken rather than remembered (FORMATS.md, *Segmenting the CODE*).
#
# Retro68's Elf2Mac takes a segment map with --mac-segments and patches
# _LoadSeg at run time (libretro/MultiSegApp.c), so a segment is read from the
# application's own resource fork the first time something in it is called.
# Segments are numbered from 3; everything unmatched lands in segment 2, and
# the Retro68 runtime is always segment 1.
#
#   cmake -B /tmp/segbuild -S mac \
#         -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$RETRO68/m68k-apple-macos/cmake/retro68.toolchain.cmake \
#         -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
#         -DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS="-Wl,--mac-segments,mac/segments.map"
#
# The harness builds its own application and passes no linker flags, so the
# way to put a segmented build in front of the emulator is LDFLAGS, which
# CMake seeds CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS from at CONFIGURE time — with a fresh
# build directory, or the first configuration is kept for ever:
#
#   rm -rf build/mac-autotest
#   LDFLAGS="-Wl,--mac-segments,mac/segments.map" \
#       tools/vmac/run.py --mem 1M fixtures/<fixture>
#
# CHECK THE BINARY BEFORE BELIEVING THE RUN: a flag that did not arrive leaves
# a single CODE 2 and a green run that measured nothing. A segmented build has
# eight CODE resources.

SEGMENT Archives
